{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients with triple-negative breast cancer (ER-, PR-, HER2-) exhibit a higher tumor mutational burden and a distinctive global gene expression profile compared to hormone receptor-positive subtypes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare TMB and global expression patterns between triple-negative and hormone receptor-positive breast cancer patients.",  
      "steps":  
        "1. Load clinical, mutation, and gene expression datasets.\n"  
        ,"deliverables":"1. Data frame of subtype labels, TMB, and expression PCA coordinates.\n2. Statistical comparison of TMB (Mann–Whitney U test).\n3. Unsupervised PCA or hierarchical clustering plot colored by subtype with variance explained.\n4. Volcano plot or heatmap of top differentially expressed genes between subtypes."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"High tumor expression of immune checkpoint genes CD274 (PD-L1) and CTLA4 is associated with longer overall survival independently of estrogen receptor status.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether patients with high PD-L1 or CTLA4 expression have improved survival, adjusting for ER status.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression datasets; extract CD274 and CTLA4 expression per patient.\n2. Merge with ER status and survival times (days_to_death or days_to_last_followup) and vital_status.\n3. Dichotomize expression into high vs low (e.g., above vs below median).\n4. Fit multivariable Cox proportional hazards model with expression group and ER status as covariates.\n5. Plot Kaplan–Meier curves stratified by expression group.\n6. Test proportional hazards assumption.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Cox model summary table with hazard ratios and p-values.\n2. KM curves for high vs low expression.\n3. Table of median survival times per group."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Somatic mutations in PIK3CA are associated with upregulation of PI3K pathway genes in tumor expression profiles.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether PIK3CA-mutant tumors show higher expression of core PI3K signaling genes than wild-type tumors.",  
      "steps":"1. Load mutation and gene expression datasets; label each patient as PIK3CA-mutant (any non-silent mutation) or wild-type.\n2. Define a PI3K pathway gene set (e.g., PIK3CA, PIK3CB, AKT1, AKT2, MTOR, PTEN).\n3. For each gene in the set, compare expression between mutant and wild-type using Mann–Whitney U tests and adjust for multiple testing.\n4. Compute a pathway activity score per sample (e.g., mean Z-score of pathway genes) and compare between groups.\n5. Visualize boxplots of pathway score by mutation status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of differential expression statistics for PI3K genes (fold-change and adjusted p-values).\n2. Boxplot of pathway activity score by PIK3CA mutation status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients who received neoadjuvant treatment present with fewer positive lymph nodes at surgery and improved disease-free survival compared to those who did not.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare lymph node involvement and disease-free survival between neoadjuvant treatment groups.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical data; extract neoadjuvant_treatment status, lymph_nodes_positive, days_to_new_tumor, new_tumor_event.\n2. Create two cohorts: received neoadjuvant treatment vs not.\n3. Compare lymph_nodes_positive counts across groups using Mann–Whitney U test.\n4. For disease-free survival, define time to new tumor event or censor at last followup.\n5. Generate Kaplan–Meier curves stratified by treatment group and test difference via log-rank test.\n6. Adjust for age and stage via Cox model as sensitivity analysis.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Statistical test results for lymph node count comparison.\n2. KM curves for disease-free survival with log-rank p-value.\n3. Cox model summary adjusting for covariates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Positive surgical margins predict a higher probability of a new tumor event within five years post-surgery, independent of pathologic stage and adjuvant therapy.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the risk of new tumor events associated with surgical margin status.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical data; extract surgical_margin, new_tumor_event, days_to_new_tumor, pathologic_stage, pharmaceutical_therapy, radiation_therapy.\n2. Exclude patients with missing margin or outcome data.\n3. Fit a logistic regression model predicting new_tumor_event (yes/no) from margin status, adjusting for stage and adjuvant therapy indicators.\n4. Report odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals.\n5. Validate model calibration (e.g., via Hosmer–Lemeshow test).\n6. Plot predicted probabilities by margin status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary table with adjusted odds ratios.\n2. Calibration plot or goodness-of-fit statistic.\n3. Table of predicted probabilities per surgical margin group."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"African American patients exhibit distinct gene expression signatures and PAM50 subtype distributions compared to White patients in TCGA-BRCA.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Characterize racial differences in molecular breast cancer subtypes and global expression patterns.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression datasets; subset patients self-reported as 'BLACK OR AFRICAN AMERICAN' or 'WHITE'.\n2. Compute PAM50 subtype calls per patient using a published gene signature and nearest-centroid classification.\n3. Compare subtype frequencies between races with chi-square test.\n4. Perform differential expression analysis genome-wide between the two racial groups (e.g., limma or DESeq2 framework) controlling for age and stage.\n5. Identify top differentially expressed genes and pathways via enrichment analysis.\n6. Visualize subtype barplots and volcano plot of DE results.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of PAM50 subtype counts by race and chi-square p-value.\n2. List of top DE genes with adjusted p-values.\n3. Volcano plot and pathway enrichment report."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Older age at diagnosis is associated with a lower burden of high-impact somatic mutations (IMPACT = HIGH) in breast tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test correlation between patient age and count of predicted high-impact somatic mutations.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and mutation datasets; extract age_at_diagnosis and filter mutations with IMPACT == 'HIGH'.\n2. Count number of HIGH-impact mutations per patient.\n3. Merge counts with age_at_diagnosis; exclude missing values.\n4. Compute Spearman rank correlation between age and high-impact mutation count.\n5. Fit a linear regression model on log-transformed counts vs age to estimate effect size.\n6. Plot scatterplot with smoothing line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Spearman correlation coefficient and p-value.\n2. Regression coefficient with confidence interval.\n3. Scatterplot of age vs high-impact mutation count."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher AJCC pathologic N stage (lymph node involvement) is associated with elevated expression of genes involved in epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether increasing nodal stage correlates with EMT gene signature activity.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression datasets; extract pathologic_n and map to ordinal values (e.g., N0=0, N1=1, N2=2, N3=3).\n2. Define an EMT gene set (e.g., CDH1, VIM, SNAI1, ZEB1, TWIST1).\n3. Calculate an EMT signature score per sample (e.g., mean expression of mesenchymal markers minus epithelial markers).\n4. Test for trend using Spearman correlation between nodal stage ordinal and EMT score.\n5. Alternatively, group patients by N stage and compare signature scores across groups via Kruskal–Wallis test.\n6. Visualize boxplots or trend lines of signature by nodal stage.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Correlation statistics or test for trend.\n2. Boxplots of EMT score by N stage.\n3. Table of mean signature scores per group."  
    }  
  }  
]}
